<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mer-Égée</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1797–1799</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mer-Égée ('<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean Sea</a>') was a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> created in 1797 from the southern Ionian Islands, formerly Venetian, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-campo-formio">Treaty of Campo Formio</a>. Centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakynthos">Zakynthos</a> and including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kythira">Kythira</a>, it was one of three departments, with Corcyre and Ithaque, into which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> organised the islands. A Russian-Ottoman expedition ended French rule there in 1798–1799.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mer-egee.jpg" alt="Mer-Égée" width="320" height="326" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SeptinsularRepublic1801.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
